om】\nL27:     * 【13†Merch†merriamwebster.threadless.com】\nL28: \nL29: 【14†Dictionary】 Definition\nL30: \nL31:   * # petition\nL32: \nL33: 1 of 2\nL34: ## 【15†noun】\nL35: \nL36: pe·​ti·​tion 【16†pə-ˈti-shən 】\nL37: \nL38: plural petitions\nL39: \nL40: 【17†Synonyms of petition】\nL41: \nL42: Simple Definition A Simple Definition is available from our Learner's Dictionary to help you understand the meaning faster.\nL43: \nL44: 1\nL45: \nL46: a\nL47: \nL48: : a formal written request made to an authority or organized body (such as a court)\nL49: \nL50: filing a petition for divorce\nL51: \nL52: Her defense lawyer had petitioned the court to videotape Scott's testimony about Hearst before he died, but the petition was denied.—Robert Lipsyte\nL53: \nL54: b\nL55: : a written request or call for change signed by many people in support of a shared cause or concern\nL56: \nL57: Hundreds of contributors and readers signed a petition circulated by the National Book Critics Circle, urging the Post to save the stand-alone section.—Motoko Rich\nL58: \nL59: Her group put together a petition signed by more than 100,000 consumers demanding that regulators crack down on companies who \"robocall\" consumers using automated dialers and recorded voices to market scams.—Shawn Zeller\nL60: \nL61: 2\nL62: : an earnest request : 【18†entreaty】\nL63: \nL64: … he begged to sleep with Hareton, and his petition was granted for once.—Emily Brontë\nL65: \nL66: 3\nL67: \nL68: : something asked or requested\nL69: \nL70: petitionary\nL71: \nL72: 【19†pə-ˈti-shə-ˌner-ē 】\nL73: \nL74: adjective\nL75: \nL76: petition\nL77: \nL78: 2 of 2\nL79: ## 【20†verb】\nL80: \nL81: petitioned; petitioning 【21†pə-ˈti-sh(ə-)niŋ 】 ; petitions\nL82: \nL83: 1\nL84: \nL85: 【22†transitive】 : to make a request to (someone)\nL86: \nL87: especially : to make a formal written request to (an authority)\nL88: \nL89: His people petitioned the government for permission to use the steps of the Lincoln Memorial. —Taylor Branch\nL90: \nL91: … to adopt \"red-flag\" laws, which allow police officers and family members to petition a court to bar dangerous individuals from possessing guns. —Norman J. Ornstein\nL92: \nL93: 2\nL94: \nL95: 【23†intransitive】 : to make a request\nL96: She neither petitioned for her right nor claimed it.—George Meredith\nL97: \nL98: especially : to make a formal written request\nL99: \nL100: A year later he moved to Rome and petitioned to join the Jesuits, who gave him a university education and ordained him in 1586. —Brian Vickers\nL101: ## Synonyms of petition\nL102: \nL103: Relevance\nL104: \nL105: Noun\nL106: \nL107:   * 【24†plea】\nL108:   * 【25†appeal】\nL109:   * 【26†prayer】\nL110: \nL111: Verb\nL112: \nL113:   * 【27†beg】\nL114: \nL115: 【17†See All Synonyms & Antonyms in Thesaurus】\nL116: ## Examples of petition in a Sentence\nL117: \nL118: Noun They collected 2,000 signatures on a petition demanding that women be allowed to join the club. Would you like to sign our petition? We presented a petition to the legislature to change the law. She filed a petition for divorce. We ask you to hear our petition. Verb The organization petitioned the government to investigate the issue. All people had the right to petition the king for help. She petitioned to join their club. See More\nL119: \nL120: Recent Examples on the Web\nL121: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【28†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL122: \nL123: Noun\nL124: The question is going to voters because opponents gathered petition signatures forcing a referendum. — Arkansas Online, 26 Oct. 2026 Attorneys for Pro Silver Star sent the band a cease-and-desist letter on Thursday, the filing states, and a copy of the petition for the restraining order and injunction on Friday. — Chuck Schilken, Los Angeles Times, 26 Oct. 2026\nL125: \nL126: Verb\nL127: Some are petitioning the State to dissolve the Union, to disregard the requisitions of the President. — René Ostberg, Encyclopedia Britannica, 30 Oct. 2026 New Jersey petitioned the Supreme Court to resolve the circuit split, and 39 other states and the District of Columbia filed a brief supporting New Jersey’s petition. — Jon Brodkin, ArsTechnica, 29 Oct. 2026 【29†See All Example Sentences for petition】\nL128: ## Word History\nL129: \nL130: Etymology\nL131: \nL132: Noun\nL133: \nL134: Middle English, from Anglo-French, from Latin petition-, petitio, from petere to seek, request — more at 【30†feather】\nL135: \nL136: First Known Use\nL137: \nL138: Noun\nL139: \nL140: 14th century, in the meaning defined at 【31†sense 2】\nL141: \nL142: Verb\nL143: \nL144: 1607, in the meaning defined at 【32†sense 1】\nL145: \nL146: Time Traveler\nL147: \nL148: The first known use of petition was in the 14th century\nL149: \nL150: 【33†See more words from the same century】\nL151: \nL152: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL153: \nL154: 【34†petitgrain oil】\nL155: \nL156: petition\nL157: \nL158: 【35†petitional】\nL159: \nL160: 【36†See all Nearby Words】\nL161: ## Cite this Entry\nL162: \nL163: Style\nL164: \nL165: [Select]\nL166: \nL167: “Petition.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/petition. Accessed 2 Nov. 2026.\nL168: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDEMONSTRATION Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster (https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/demonstration)\nciteturn24view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/demonstration\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 266\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】 【1†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: [Input: Search Dictionary]\nL3: \nL4:   * 【2†Games】\nL5:   * 【3†Word of the Day】\nL6:   * 【4†Grammar】\nL7:   * 【5†Word Finder】\nL8:   * 【6†Slang】\nL9:   * 【7†Newsletters】\n