 in Thesaurus】\nL96: ## Examples of demonstration in a Sentence\nL97: I went to a sculpture demonstration last weekend. One of the instructors gave a demonstration of how to prune a tree. Would you mind giving us a demonstration so that we can see how the machine works? He brought along a copy of the software for demonstration. Students took part in several nonviolent demonstrations against the government. The latest tests are a clear demonstration that the vaccine works. Many people sent cards and flowers in demonstration of their sympathy. See More\nL98: Recent Examples on the Web\nL99: \nL100: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【24†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL101: Soldiers spread out in Milan and luxury boutiques were boarded up in the Italian fashion and finance capital as protesters launched fresh demonstrations on Saturday, one week after looting and clashes with police. — ABC News, 24 Oct. 2026 There is a history of WNBA teams staying off the court as a demonstration. — Dan Zaksheske Outkick, FOXNews.com, 24 Oct. 2026 The Paris prosecutor’s office said 64 people had been arrested in connection with Saturday’s demonstration and 40 placed in police custody.\nL102: — Richard Engel, NBC news, 24 Oct. 2026 Some even pitched tents and 55 people were arrested in the early hours of Tuesday morning, prompting further demonstrations. — Issy Ronald, CNN Money, 24 Oct. 2026 【25†See All Example Sentences for demonstration】\nL103: ## Word History\nL104: \nL105: Etymology\nL106: \nL107: Middle English demonstracioun, borrowed from Anglo-French & Latin; Anglo-French demonstration, demustracioun, borrowed from Latin dēmonstrātiōn-, dēmonstrātiō \"action of pointing out, description, explanation\" (Late Latin, \"deduction, proof\"), from dēmonstrāre \"to indicate, describe, show\" + -tiōn-, -tiō, suffix of verbal action — more at 【26†demonstrate】\nL108: \nL109: First Known Use\nL110: \nL111: 14th century, in the meaning defined at 【27†sense 1】\nL112: \nL113: Time Traveler\nL114: The first known use of demonstration was in the 14th century\nL115: \nL116: 【28†See more words from the same century】\nL117: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL118: \nL119: 【26†demonstrate】\nL120: \nL121: demonstration\nL122: \nL123: 【29†demonstrational】\nL124: \nL125: 【30†See all Nearby Words】\nL126: \nL127: ## Cite this Entry\nL128: \nL129: Style\nL130: \nL131: [Select]\nL132: \nL133: “Demonstration.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/demonstration. Accessed 2 Nov. 2026.\nL134: \nL135: 【31†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】Copy Citation\nL136: ## Kids Definition\nL137: \nL138: demonstration\nL139: \nL140: noun\nL141: \nL142: dem·​on·​stra·​tion 【16†ˌdem-ən-ˈstrā-shən 】\nL143: \nL144: 1\nL145: \nL146: : an act, process, or means of demonstrating the truth of something:\nL147: \nL148: a\nL149: \nL150: : convincing evidence\nL151: \nL152: b\nL153: \nL154: : an explanation (as of a theory) by experiment\nL155: \nL156: c\nL157: \nL158: : a course of reasoning intended to prove that a conclusion must be true when certain conditions are accepted\nL159: \nL160: d\nL161: \nL162: : a showing or using of a product for sale to display its good points\nL163: \nL164: 2\nL165: \nL166: : an outward expression or display\nL167: \nL168: a demonstration of joy\nL169: \nL170: 3\nL171: : a show of armed force\nL172: \nL173: 4\nL174: \nL175: : a public display of group feelings toward a person or cause\nL176: ## Medical Definition\nL177: \nL178: demonstration\nL179: \nL180: noun\nL181: \nL182: dem·​on·​stra·​tion 【16†ˌdem-ən-ˈstrā-shən 】\nL183: \nL184: : an act, process, or means of 【32†demonstrating】 to the intelligence\nL185: \nL186: especially : a proof by experiment\nL187: \nL188: a demonstration of the neutralization of an acid by a base\n"
